"Parallax: Measuring the Distance to a Star | VirtualDemos",
"Parallax: Measuring the Distance to a Star",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> {#} Parallax; Spectroscopic parallax; Main sequence fitting; {#} Cepheids; Cepheid photometry; {#} Type Ia; {#} Hubble’s law; Whole ladder. This worksheet uses {#} Parallax.",
"On the left, Earth’s orbit around the Sun with a nearby star beyond it, in 3D (drag the empty sky to turn it) or, with Flat, seen from above; the angles are hugely exaggerated so you can see them. Drag Earth around its orbit, or play a year.",
"On the right, what a telescope sees: a photo in January, a photo in July, and the two laid on top of each other. The nearby star sits in a different place against the far background stars in the two photos.",
"Choose a real star (Proxima Centauri, Sirius, Vega) or Any distance, then set the distance with the slider. Choose the instrument: Ground (telescopes on the ground before the 1990s), Hipparcos (a satellite, {#} to {#}) or Gaia (a satellite that measured from {#} to {#}). Each can place a star only to within its position uncertainty, drawn as a dashed circle when it is big enough to see.",
"Drag the two ends of the ruler in the overlaid photo onto the January and July images to measure the shift.",
"Angles are in arcseconds (″) and milliarcseconds (mas): {#}″ is {#}/{#} of a degree, and {#} mas is {#}″.",
"Record reading stores the star, the instrument, the distance, the parallax, the January to July shift, your ruler’s shift and the distance from it, and the distance uncertainty. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the Data table below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Measure the distance to nearby stars the way astronomers do, from the small shift in a star’s position as Earth goes around the Sun, and find out how far this method can reach.",
"<strong>Parallax.</strong> Hold a finger up at arm’s length and look at it with one eye, then the other: it jumps against the background. The closer the finger, the bigger the jump. Earth does the same thing for us on a much bigger scale: in January and July it is on opposite sides of the Sun, {#} AU apart, so a nearby star seems to shift against much more distant stars. Half of that shift is the star’s <strong>parallax</strong>, p: the angle the radius of Earth’s orbit ({#} AU) makes when seen from the star.",
"<strong>The parsec.</strong> Astronomers define the distance at which p is exactly {#}″ as one <strong>parsec</strong> (pc): {#} pc = {#} AU = {#} light years. Because the angles are tiny, the distance is simply",
"distance in parsecs = {#} ÷ (parallax in arcseconds) = {#} ÷ (parallax in mas).",
"A star twice as far has half the parallax.",
"<strong>How far it works.</strong> Every instrument can place a star only to within some small angle, its position uncertainty. When the parallax is not much bigger than that, the distance is poorly known. The share of the distance that is uncertain equals the share of the parallax that is uncertain: (uncertainty in p) ÷ p.",
"Hold your thumb at arm’s length and blink one eye, then the other. Now hold it halfway. Does it jump more or less? How does that match the rule “twice as far, half the parallax”?",
"A star has a parallax of {#}″. How far is it in parsecs? In light years?",
"Guess: is the nearest star’s parallax bigger or smaller than the size of the full Moon in the sky (about {#}″, just over half a degree)? By how much, roughly?",
"Proxima Centauri is too faint to see without a telescope, while Sirius and Vega are two of the brightest stars in the night sky. Which of the three do you expect to have the largest parallax, and which the smallest? Give a reason.",
"<strong>Part {#}: three nearby stars.</strong> With the {#} Parallax setup on, press Proxima Centauri under Star and Hipparcos under Instrument. In the overlaid photo, drag one end of the ruler onto the center of the January image of the nearby star and the other end onto the center of its July image; the shift appears in the Your ruler readout. Press Record reading. Do the same for Sirius and for Vega.",
"<strong>Part {#}: twice as far, half the angle.</strong> Press Any distance, keep Hipparcos, and set the Distance d slider to {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} pc in turn (the slider sticks at round values; the arrow keys fine tune). Press Record reading at each (you do not need the ruler here). Press Export to worksheet to send all eight readings, then Clear.",
"<strong>Part {#}: how far can it reach?</strong> Keep Any distance. Set Distance d to {#} pc and, pressing Ground, Hipparcos and Gaia in turn, press Record reading with each. Do the same at {#} pc ({#} kpc) and at {#} pc ({#} kpc). Watch the photos and the warning under the readouts as you go. Press Export to worksheet to send the nine readings; they go into a second copy of the Data table.",
