"Part {#}: compare the distance from your ruler with the demo’s distance for each star. Change each distance into light years ({#} pc = {#} light years). Proxima Centauri is the nearest star to the Sun: how long does its light take to reach us?",
"Part {#}: fill in the last column, {#} ÷ d, for the eight readings of Part {#} and Part {#}. Graph the parallax p (mas, vertical axis) against {#} ÷ d (horizontal axis). Is it a straight line through the origin? What is its slope, and what does that number mean? (Hint: what parallax would a star at {#} pc have?)",
"Part {#}: the uncertainty in p stays the same while p shrinks as {#} ÷ d, so the uncertainty in the distance grows in step with the distance: ten times farther, ten times the uncertainty. For each instrument, use one of your readings to work out the distance at which its uncertainty is {#} %. Ground’s readings say not measurable, so for Ground use its position uncertainty, {#} mas: the distance uncertainty is {#} % where the parallax is ten times that, {#} mas. That is how far each instrument can reach with good distances.",
"The Sun is about {#} kpc from the center of our galaxy, and the galaxy’s disk is about {#} kpc across. Which instrument can measure parallax distances across a good part of the galaxy? Can any of them reach another galaxy, such as Andromeda, about {#} million light years ({#} kpc) away? What does this tell you about why astronomers need the other rungs of the distance ladder?",
